  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Arrhenius defined an acid as a substance that produces H⁺ ions in aqueous solution; the proton-donor wording is the Brønsted–Lowry definition.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A Brønsted–Lowry base accepts a proton, which is why ammonia is a base even though it contains no hydroxide ion.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Removing one proton from H₂SO₄ leaves HSO₄⁻, which is its conjugate base; removing the second proton gives SO₄²⁻.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A conjugate acid has one more proton than its base, so adding H⁺ to HCO₃⁻ gives H₂CO₃.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hydrochloric acid ionises completely in water; the other three ionise only partly and are weak acids.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A strong monobasic acid ionises completely, so [H⁺] = 0.010 = 10⁻² and pH = 2.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    pOH = −log(10⁻³) = 3, so pH = 14 − 3 = 11.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sulphuric acid is dibasic, so [H⁺] = 2 × 0.0050 = 0.010 and pH = 2.0.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    [H⁺] = 10^(−pH) = 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Each pH unit is a factor of ten, and the difference is three units, so the factor is 10³ = 1000.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A tenfold fall in concentration is one pH unit in the alkaline direction, so the pH rises by 1.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    [H⁺] = √(Ka·c) = √(10⁻⁶) = 10⁻³, so pH = 3.0.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The equivalence point is acidic, so the indicator must change in the acid range; methyl orange changes from red to yellow over pH 3.1–4.4.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Phenolphthalein is colourless below about pH 8.3 and pink above about 10.0, so at pH 5 it is colourless.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Methyl orange changes from red to yellow over pH 3.1–4.4, so at pH 10 it is fully yellow.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The acid and the base react 1 : 1 and the volumes are equal, so the concentrations are equal.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Moles of acid = 0.0010; the equation needs two moles of NaOH per mole of acid, so moles of base = 0.0020, and c = 0.0020 ÷ 0.025 = 0.080 M.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂; moles of carbonate = 0.0050, so acid needed = 0.0100 mol, which is 100 cm³ of 0.100 M acid.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Barium sulphate is the standard insoluble sulphate; sodium, ammonium and potassium salts are soluble.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Barium sulphate is insoluble and precipitates at once; every other pair listed contains only soluble combinations.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Silver chloride is insoluble and forms a white precipitate that darkens in light.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lead(II) iodide is the familiar yellow precipitate; lead(II) chloride is white.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Titration is used when both the acid and the base are soluble and the salt contains Na, K or NH₄; sodium chloride is the example.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Copper(II) oxide is insoluble, so excess is added and filtered off; the filtrate is then evaporated and cooled to crystallise.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The two gases combine directly to form the white solid, which is the method the syllabus names for this salt.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The water of crystallisation is driven off to leave the white anhydrous salt; further strong heating would decompose it.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    M = 160 + 90 = 250, and 90 ÷ 250 × 100 = 36 %.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Moles of salt = 1.60 ÷ 160 = 0.0100; moles of water = 0.90 ÷ 18 = 0.0500; the ratio is 5.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The carbonate ion accepts a proton from water and leaves hydroxide ions, so the solution is basic.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺; the hydrogen ion produced makes the solution acidic.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sodium chloride is the salt of a strong acid and a strong base, so neither ion reacts with water.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Aluminium oxide reacts with both acids and alkalis, which is the definition of amphoteric.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A metal above hydrogen in the reactivity series displaces hydrogen from a dilute acid.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Carbon dioxide is evolved, and it turns limewater milky by forming calcium carbonate.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Neutralisation is the combination of hydrogen ions and hydroxide ions to form water.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Basicity is the number of replaceable hydrogen atoms per molecule, and phosphoric acid has three.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Every ion in the last pair forms a soluble salt, so no precipitate appears.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    An impurity lowers the melting point and spreads it over a range; a pure substance melts sharply.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    n = 4.0 ÷ 40 = 0.10 mol in 0.500 dm³, so c = 0.20 mol dm⁻³.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    pH + pOH = 14, so pOH = 14 − 11 = 3.
  ]

]

